  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Adopting Innovative Menstrual Hygiene Management In Acute Settings</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Our project, which has reached Level 3 on NESTA&apos;s Standard of Evidence through a Randomised Control Trial (RCT) by Reemi, aims to progress to Level 4, focusing on the applicability of these findings in emergency situations. Early evidence from a pilot in Vanuatu indicates Reemi products are inclusive for people with intellectual &amp; physical disabilities. We plan to integrate the distribution of Reemi&apos;s Menstrual Hygiene Management (MHM) products as a principal component of our broader humanitarian intervention in Gaza. Critical to this will be to ensure adequate menstrual materials, sanitation facilities, and access to hygiene resources in emergencies. As experts in Water Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH), we are well equipped to address these needs. In addition, effective MHM in crises goes beyond distributing products; it requires education to reduce the associated psychosocial impacts. Our approach will include community education to enhance menstrual knowledge and product accessibility.</narrative>
  </description>
